l. children with disabilities;
m. child victims of maltreatment and neglect; n. children with deviant social behavior; ando. children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.
2. Special Protection for Children is carried out through efforts to:a. provide rapid handling, including treatment and/or physical, psychological, and social rehabilitation, as well as prevention of diseases and other health disorders;
b. provide psychosocial assistance during treatment until recovery;
c. provide social assistance for Children from families who are unable; andd. provide protection and assistance in every judicial process.
3. Special Protection for Children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be provided at technical implementing units of ministries/agencies, Regional Apparatus, and/or regional technical implementing units that have been established with reference to established service standards.
4. Special Protection for Children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented quickly, comprehensively, and in an integrated manner.

Article 29

( 1 ) The Regency Government is obliged to fulfill children's rights in the fields of:
a. civil rights and freedoms;
b. family environment and alternative care; c. basic health and welfare; andd. education, use of leisure time, and arts and cultural activities.

( 2 ) In fulfilling children's rights as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a, the Regency Government is obliged to:
a. endeavor for children to obtain birth certificates free of charge;
b. provide infrastructure and facilities needed by children to express themselves, think, and create; andc. provide infrastructure and facilities so that children can obtain the knowledge they need.
3. In fulfilling children's rights as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, the Regency Government undertakes efforts to:a. provide guidance to families or substitute families to fulfill the rights of children required during their care;
b. provide family consultation institutions for child caregivers;
c. provide child social welfare institutions as an alternative place for child caregivers in the event that families or substitute families cannot provide proper care; andd. provide guidance to children as a vulnerable group.
4. In fulfilling children's rights as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c, the Regency Government undertakes efforts to: -18-a. reduce infant mortality rates;
b. reduce malnutrition rates for children;
c. provide breast milk for infants;
d. provide immunization for children;
e. provide reproductive health services;
f. increase access to welfare improvement for poor families;
g. increase access to clean water for households; h. create smoke-free zones; andi. child-friendly community health centers.
5. In fulfilling children's rights as referred to in paragraph (1) letter d, the Regency Government undertakes efforts to:a. provide early childhood education;
b. implement 12 years of compulsory education;
c. establish child-friendly schools;
d. establish safe routes to/from school; ande. provide child-friendly creative and recreational activity facilities;
6. Further provisions regarding the form and procedures for fulfilling children's rights as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

