Article 32

1. The Regency Government is obliged and responsible to provide special protection to child victims of violence which includes:a. children in emergency situations;
b. children in conflict with the law;
c. children from minority and isolated groups;
d. children who are economically and/or sexually exploited;
e. children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropics, and addictive substance abuse;
f. children who are victims of pornography;
g. children with HIV/AIDS;
h. child victims of kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking;
i. child victims of physical and/or mental violence;
j. child victims of sexual crimes;
k. child victims of terrorism networks;
20-
l. children with disabilities;
m. child victims of maltreatment and neglect; n. children with deviant social behavior; ando. children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.
2. Special Protection for Children is carried out through efforts to:a. provide rapid handling, including treatment and/or physical, psychological, and social rehabilitation, as well as prevention of diseases and other health disorders;
b. provide psychosocial assistance during treatment until recovery;
c. provide social assistance for Children from families who are unable; andd. provide protection and assistance in every judicial process.
3. Special Protection for Children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be provided at technical implementing units of ministries/agencies, Regional Apparatus, and/or regional technical implementing units that have been established with reference to established service standards.
4. Special Protection for Children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented quickly, comprehensively, and in an integrated manner.

Article 64

Prevention efforts as referred to in Article 62 shall be carried out in the form of:
a. formulating and implementing policies related to action plans on the prevention and handling of Children with HIV and AIDS;
b. health promotion to increase public knowledge about the benefits of early detection and transmission of HIV and AIDS as well as to increase the knowledge and responsibility of mothers, pregnant mothers, Children, and married couples;
c. preventing the transmission of HIV and AIDS from mother to Child; d. preventing Children from using narcotics;
e. early detection by providing HIV and AIDS tests to pregnant mothers in areas with widespread and concentrated HIV and AIDS endemics;
f. early detection by providing HIV and AIDS tests to pregnant mothers with sexually transmitted infections and tuberculosis in areas with low HIV epidemics;
g. using sterile medical equipment for Children to avoid HIV and AIDS;
h. avoiding blood transfusions contaminated with HIV and AIDS to Children;
i. providing information about HIV and AIDS transmission to Children;
j. changing Children's behavior to avoid HIV and AIDS;
k. keeping Children away from tattooing; andl. establishing communication, consultation, and information centers on HIV and AIDS at the village level.

Article 51

Prevention efforts as referred to in Article 49 shall be carried out through:
a. communication, information, and education about the dangers for Children if involved in production and distribution as well as the dangers of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropics, and other addictive substances;
b. enhancement of the role of parents, families, the Community, educators, education personnel, religious leaders, customary leaders, Community leaders in supporting the Social Reintegration process of Children Who Are Victims of Narcotics, Alcohol, Psychotropic, and Other Addictive Substance Abuse;
c. provision of understanding and awareness to Children regarding the dangers of smoking;
d. provision of understanding and awareness to Children about the dangers of Children being involved in production and distribution as well as the dangers of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropics, and other addictive substances;
e. involvement of Children as peers in order to provide understanding and change of mindset about the dangers of Children being involved in production and distribution as well as the dangers of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropics, and other addictive substances; andf. inclusion of anti-narcotics, alcohol, psychotropics, and other addictive substances teaching materials in education units.

